{pad:ignore}
<p><code>{pad:foo}</code> has no use of its own: it answers the line <code>Foo tag from pad</code>.
It exists so that the manual can show what a name resolves to when several kinds of thing
carry it - an application tag in <code>_tags/foo.php</code>, a pipe function, a PHP function,
a data or bool store, a content block.</p>

<p>The required prefix selects the kind: <code>{pad:foo}</code> is the built-in tag,
<code>{app:foo}</code> the application's, and <code>php:</code>, <code>function:</code>,
<code>data:</code> and <code>bool:</code> select their own namespaces. The second example
has an application tag in <code>_tags/foo.php</code>: <code>app:foo</code> reaches that file
and <code>pad:foo</code> reaches the built-in.</p>
{/pad:ignore}
